              "Infrastructure as Code means your entire AI platform — VPCs, subnets, security groups, GPU instance groups, S3 buckets, IAM roles, container registries, and managed ML services — is defined in version-controlled code. Changes go through pull request review before touching infrastructure. Drift between environments is eliminated. An entire production ML platform that took three weeks to manually configure can be reproduced in 15 minutes by running terraform apply.",
              "Terraform uses HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language) to declare the desired state of infrastructure. Key concepts: providers (cloud-specific plugins for AWS, GCP, Azure), resources (actual cloud resources like aws_instance or google_container_cluster), data sources (read existing infrastructure you don't manage), variables (parameterize configurations), outputs (expose values for use by other modules), and modules (reusable infrastructure components). A well-structured Terraform codebase uses modules for ML platform components that teams can reuse.",
              "The Terraform workflow: `terraform init` downloads providers and initializes the backend. `terraform plan` generates an execution plan showing exactly what will change — this is your diff before touching production. `terraform apply` executes the plan. `terraform destroy` tears down all resources. In CI/CD pipelines, `terraform plan` output is posted as a PR comment for review, and `terraform apply` runs automatically after approval.",
              "Remote state is essential for teams. The state file tracks what Terraform has created — it's the source of truth that maps Terraform code to real cloud resources. Local state breaks in team environments (conflicts when two engineers run apply simultaneously). Remote state on S3 (with DynamoDB for locking) or Terraform Cloud ensures only one apply runs at a time and the state is durable. State files may contain sensitive values and must never be committed to git.",
              "A complete ML platform Terraform module typically provisions: VPC with public and private subnets, NAT gateway for private subnet internet access, S3 buckets for training data and model artifacts (with versioning and encryption), IAM roles for training jobs and inference services, ECR repositories for Docker images, EKS cluster with GPU and CPU node groups, and SageMaker domain — all reproducible with a single command."
