              "Production AI Dockerfiles follow strict best practices. Start from official NVIDIA CUDA base images (nvidia/cuda:11.8.0-runtime-ubuntu22.04) for GPU support. Use multi-stage builds to separate the build environment from the runtime image — dramatically reducing final image size. Copy requirements.txt and install dependencies before copying application code, leveraging Docker's layer caching so a code change doesn't re-install all Python packages. Pin exact dependency versions. Run as a non-root user. Use .dockerignore to exclude training data, notebooks, and test files.",
              "Container registries are the distribution layer for AI Docker images. AWS ECR (Elastic Container Registry), Google Artifact Registry, and Azure Container Registry provide enterprise-grade storage with IAM integration, vulnerability scanning, image signing, and geo-replication. ECR lifecycle policies automatically delete old image versions — preventing storage cost accumulation. Your CI/CD pipeline builds, tests, scans for vulnerabilities, and pushes new images on every merge to main.",
              "Volumes and bind mounts are critical patterns for AI containers. Model weights of 10-40GB should never be baked into Docker images — they make images enormous and slow to pull. Instead, store weights in S3/GCS and mount them at container startup via EFS, GCS FUSE, or a custom init container pattern. For local development, bind mounts reflect code changes instantly without rebuilding, while volumes provide persistent storage for databases and caches.",
              "Docker Compose orchestrates multi-container AI development environments. A typical ML service locally runs: a FastAPI inference server, Redis for feature caching, PostgreSQL for prediction logging, and a Prometheus/Grafana stack for monitoring — all defined in docker-compose.yml and started with one command. This local environment is a faithful replica of production, dramatically reducing integration bugs discovered only after deployment."
